From c19b14865b6d713a538a1a8e90f5cd0727ce35c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Youwes09 <yuvi.shen@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:55:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] stub: Decrypt AEA-encrypted recovery images

From macOS 26 the BaseSystem image in a restore image is an Apple
Encrypted Archive (.dmg.aea). Copied as it is, the stub's recovery
volume holds an image that cannot be attached, and firmware collection
fails after the disk has already been partitioned.

Decrypt it with macOS's own aea tool, using the key Apple publishes for
the archive on its public key server, and store the plain image as
before. Only known archives are accepted, with the encrypted and
decrypted SHA-256 pinned, so each new restore image is added
deliberately. Decryption runs right after the restore image is chosen,
before anything on disk changes, so a bad download or key stops the
install cleanly.

load_identity() no longer sets the stub's paths, which need the target
volume; install_files() does that itself, and the repair and firmware
rebuild paths already do through check_existing_install().

The approach, including decrypting before partitioning, and the 26.5.1
key and hashes come from the Gravity Linux installer (MIT) as shipped
by Omarchy-M4-Native.
